    ("q-ai-nn-006", "c-ai-nn-001", "Intuitive", "Why might a network with exclusively 'dying ReLUs' fail to learn?", ["Because the weights are constantly updated to larger and larger values.", "Because the gradients become exactly zero for those neurons.", "Because the neurons cease to update during backpropagation.", "Because the learning rate automatically drops to zero.", "Because the network effectively loses capacity as neurons become inactive."], [1, 2, 4], "Dying ReLUs occur when neurons constantly output zero, meaning their gradient is zero. They stop updating during backpropagation, effectively reducing the network's capacity.", 1.2, 1.5),
    ("q-ai-nn-007", "c-ai-nn-001", "Intuitive", "Why is non-linearity crucial for the hidden layers of a neural network?", ["It prevents the network from simply collapsing into a single linear transformation.", "It allows the network to partition the input space with complex, curved decision boundaries.", "It reduces the computational cost of the forward pass.", "It guarantees that the loss function will be strictly convex.", "It enables the network to approximate any continuous function, given sufficient width."], [0, 1, 4], "Non-linearity prevents layer collapse, allows complex decision boundaries, and is necessary for the Universal Approximation Theorem. It does not reduce computational cost or guarantee a convex loss function.", 1.4, 1.6),
    ("q-ai-nn-008", "c-ai-nn-001", "Apply", "You are designing a binary classification output layer. Which combinations of activation and loss function are standard choices?", ["Sigmoid activation with Binary Cross-Entropy loss.", "Softmax activation (with 2 output nodes) with Categorical Cross-Entropy loss.", "ReLU activation with Mean Squared Error loss.", "Tanh activation with Binary Cross-Entropy loss.", "Linear activation with Hinge loss."], [0, 1, 4], "Binary classification typically uses a single node with Sigmoid + Binary Cross-Entropy, two nodes with Softmax + Categorical Cross-Entropy, or a Linear node + Hinge loss (like an SVM).", 2.0, 1.7),

