    ("q-ai-nn-009", "c-ai-nn-002", "Repeat", "Which of the following describes the Backpropagation algorithm?", ["It computes the gradient of the loss function with respect to the network weights.", "It applies the chain rule of calculus from the output layer back to the input layer.", "It is a biologically plausible mechanism for learning in the human brain.", "It updates the weights to maximize the loss function.", "It relies on dynamic programming to efficiently compute gradients by reusing intermediate derivatives."], [0, 1, 4], "Backpropagation efficiently computes gradients by applying the chain rule backwards, reusing intermediate terms (a form of dynamic programming). It is generally not considered biologically plausible and minimizes loss.", -1.1, 1.1),
    ("q-ai-nn-010", "c-ai-nn-002", "Repeat", "What are characteristics of Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)?", ["It computes the gradient using the entire training dataset at once.", "It updates weights using a single training example or a small mini-batch at each step.", "It generally exhibits more variance in its parameter updates compared to Batch Gradient Descent.", "It guarantees convergence to the global minimum for non-convex functions.", "It can escape shallow local minima more easily than Batch Gradient Descent due to update noise."], [1, 2, 4], "SGD uses mini-batches, introducing noise (variance) that can help escape shallow local minima. It does not guarantee global convergence for non-convex functions like deep neural networks.", -1.0, 1.2),
