    ("q-ai-nn-012", "c-ai-nn-002", "Summary", "How do optimizers like Adam and RMSprop differ from standard SGD?", ["They maintain per-parameter learning rates instead of a single global learning rate.", "They require significantly less memory to run.", "They use moving averages of past gradients or squared gradients to adjust updates.", "They completely eliminate the need to tune the initial learning rate hyperparameter.", "They generally converge faster on complex, non-convex loss surfaces."], [0, 2, 4], "Adaptive optimizers adjust learning rates per parameter using moving averages of past gradients, often leading to faster convergence. They do not eliminate hyperparameter tuning and actually require MORE memory to store historical states.", 0.3, 1.4),
