    ("q-ai-nn-014", "c-ai-nn-002", "Intuitive", "In the context of backpropagation, why does the vanishing gradient problem disproportionately affect earlier layers of deep networks?", ["Because the chain rule involves multiplying many derivatives less than 1 together.", "Because earlier layers have more parameters than later layers.", "Because the loss is calculated at the output, and the signal degrades as it propagates backwards.", "Because activation functions like Sigmoid compress large inputs into a small range with small derivatives.", "Because optimizers deliberately reduce the learning rate for earlier layers."], [0, 2, 3], "Gradients vanish because multiplying many small derivatives (e.g., from Sigmoid) via the chain rule causes the gradient signal from the output to decay exponentially as it reaches earlier layers.", 1.1, 1.5),
    ("q-ai-nn-015", "c-ai-nn-002", "Apply", "You are training a deep network and observe that the training loss fluctuates wildly and occasionally spikes upward. What are appropriate steps to take?", ["Decrease the learning rate.", "Increase the learning rate.", "Increase the batch size.", "Add gradient clipping.", "Change the loss function to Mean Absolute Error."], [0, 2, 3], "Wild fluctuations often mean the steps are too large or noisy. Decreasing learning rate, increasing batch size (to reduce variance), or adding gradient clipping can stabilize training.", 1.8, 1.6),
    ("q-ai-nn-016", "c-ai-nn-002", "Apply", "If you are using momentum in your optimizer, how does it physically behave on the loss surface?", ["It accelerates movement in directions with consistent gradients.", "It dampens oscillations in directions where gradients rapidly change sign.", "It allows the optimizer to smoothly roll over small local maxima or saddle points.", "It randomly jumps to different parts of the loss surface.", "It exactly computes the second derivative (Hessian) of the loss function."], [0, 1, 2], "Momentum accumulates a velocity vector, accelerating consistent directions and dampening oscillations, acting like a ball rolling with physical momentum over small bumps. It does not compute second derivatives.", 2.2, 1.8),

