    ("q-ai-nn-022", "c-ai-nn-003", "Intuitive", "As you progress deeper into a CNN, what typically happens to the features learned by the filters?", ["The receptive field of the filters relative to the original input image grows larger.", "The features transition from simple edges and textures to complex object parts.", "The spatial resolution of the feature maps increases.", "The filters become highly sensitive to the exact absolute position of the object.", "The feature maps become more semantic and abstract."], [0, 1, 4], "Deeper layers have larger effective receptive fields, learning complex, semantic, and abstract features (like faces) compared to early layers (edges). Spatial resolution typically decreases.", 1.0, 1.4),
    ("q-ai-nn-023", "c-ai-nn-003", "Apply", "You want to design a CNN that outputs a segmentation mask of the same spatial size as the input image. Which architectural components are necessary?", ["Max pooling layers only.", "Transposed convolutions (or upsampling layers).", "A fully connected layer at the very end.", "Skip connections (like in a U-Net architecture).", "An encoder-decoder structure."], [1, 3, 4], "Image segmentation requires reconstructing spatial resolution, typically using transposed convolutions within an encoder-decoder architecture, often aided by skip connections (U-Net). Fully connected layers destroy spatial info.", 2.1, 1.7),
