    ("q-ai-nn-034", "c-ai-nn-005", "Repeat", "In the scaled dot-product attention mechanism, what are the primary matrices involved?", ["Query (Q)", "Key (K)", "Value (V)", "State (S)", "Memory (M)"], [0, 1, 2], "Self-attention computes weights using Query, Key, and Value matrices derived from the input.", -1.2, 1.0),
    ("q-ai-nn-035", "c-ai-nn-005", "Repeat", "Why does the Transformer architecture require Positional Encoding?", ["Because the self-attention mechanism processes all tokens in parallel and is permutation invariant.", "Because the model needs to know the sequential order of words in a sentence.", "Because it replaces the need for an optimizer.", "Because recurrent layers are completely removed from the architecture.", "Because it compresses the vocabulary size."], [0, 1, 3], "Since Transformers lack recurrence and process sequences in parallel, they have no inherent concept of order. Positional encodings inject sequence order information.", -1.0, 1.1),
    ("q-ai-nn-036", "c-ai-nn-005", "Summary", "How does Self-Attention improve upon RNNs for long sequences?", ["It allows direct connections between any two tokens in the sequence, regardless of distance.", "It avoids the sequential bottleneck, allowing for highly parallelized computation.", "It uses significantly less memory during training.", "The path length for gradients to travel between any two tokens is O(1).", "It completely solves the hallucination problem in language models."], [0, 1, 3], "Self-attention connects all tokens directly (O(1) path length), bypassing the sequential bottleneck of RNNs and preventing vanishing gradients over distance. It is actually highly memory intensive (O(N^2)).", 0.3, 1.4),
    ("q-ai-nn-037", "c-ai-nn-005", "Summary", "What is the purpose of 'Multi-Head' attention?", ["It allows the model to jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces.", "It enables the model to focus on different aspects of the context (e.g., grammatical vs. semantic relationships) simultaneously.", "It splits the sequence length into smaller, manageable chunks.", "It provides multiple independent gradient paths to improve training stability.", "It reduces the dimensionality of the final output."], [0, 1, 3], "Multi-head attention uses multiple sets of Q, K, V weights, allowing the model to attend to different types of relationships (subspaces) concurrently.", 0.2, 1.3),
    ("q-ai-nn-038", "c-ai-nn-005", "Intuitive", "Why is the dot product of Q and K scaled by $1/\\sqrt{d_k}$ in scaled dot-product attention?", ["To prevent the dot products from growing too large in magnitude.", "Because large dot products push the softmax function into regions with extremely small gradients.", "To ensure the output probabilities always sum to 1.", "To normalize the input vectors to unit length.", "To counteract the vanishing gradient problem inside the softmax function."], [0, 1], "For large dimensions, dot products grow large, pushing the softmax into regions with vanishingly small gradients. Scaling brings the variance back to 1.", 1.3, 1.6),
    ("q-ai-nn-039", "c-ai-nn-005", "Apply", "You are implementing a causal language model (like GPT). How must the self-attention mechanism be modified?", ["A lower-triangular mask must be applied to the attention scores.", "The model must be prevented from 'looking ahead' at future tokens during training.", "The Query matrices must be disabled.", "The Positional Encoding must be removed.", "Future token attention scores are set to negative infinity before the softmax step."], [0, 1, 4], "Causal (autoregressive) models use a mask (setting upper triangle to negative infinity) to prevent tokens from attending to future tokens, preserving the autoregressive property.", 2.0, 1.7),
    ("q-ai-nn-040", "c-ai-nn-005", "Apply", "The $O(N^2)$ memory complexity of self-attention limits your context window. Which architectural adaptations can mitigate this?", ["Using Sparse Attention mechanisms.", "Using Linear Attention approximations (e.g., Performer).", "Replacing attention with LSTMs.", "Implementing Sliding Window attention (e.g., Longformer).", "Using larger batch sizes."], [0, 1, 3], "Sparse, Linear, and Sliding Window attention variants are explicitly designed to reduce the $O(N^2)$ complexity of standard attention.", 2.2, 1.8),

    # c-ai-nn-006 (10 questions)
    ("q-ai-nn-041", "c-ai-nn-006", "Repeat", "What is Transfer Learning in deep learning?", ["Taking a model trained on a large dataset and applying it to a different but related task.", "Using knowledge gained from one problem to improve learning on another.", "Transferring weights from a CPU to a GPU.", "Reusing pre-trained weights as a starting point instead of random initialization.", "Translating code from PyTorch to TensorFlow."], [0, 1, 3], "Transfer learning leverages knowledge (weights) learned on a source task/dataset to bootstrap learning on a target task.", -1.4, 0.9),
    ("q-ai-nn-042", "c-ai-nn-006", "Repeat", "Which of the following are common paradigms of Transfer Learning?", ["Feature Extraction (freezing base layers and training a new classifier head).", "Fine-tuning (unfreezing some or all base layers and training end-to-end).", "Zero-shot transfer.", "Randomizing all weights before transferring.", "Overfitting the source task."], [0, 1, 2], "Feature extraction, Fine-tuning, and Zero-shot transfer are all valid paradigms. Randomizing weights defeats the purpose.", -1.2, 1.0),
    ("q-ai-nn-043", "c-ai-nn-006", "Repeat", "What does it mean to 'freeze' layers during fine-tuning?", ["Setting the learning rate of those layers to zero.", "Preventing the weights of those layers from being updated during backpropagation.", "Saving the model weights to disk.", "Ensuring the gradients for those layers are not computed (e.g., requires_grad=False).", "Running the model in inference mode only."], [0, 1, 3], "Freezing layers stops their weights from updating by disabling gradient computation for those parameters during training.", -1.1, 1.1),
    ("q-ai-nn-044", "c-ai-nn-006", "Summary", "Why is Transfer Learning highly effective in computer vision (e.g., using ImageNet models)?", ["Early layers in CNNs learn general, fundamental features like edges and color blobs.", "These low-level features are universally useful across many different image domains.", "It drastically reduces the amount of labeled data required for the target task.", "It allows the model to process images of any arbitrary size without resizing.", "It eliminates the need for any backpropagation."], [0, 1, 2], "CNNs trained on ImageNet learn universal early features (edges, textures). Transferring these drastically reduces the data and compute needed for new tasks.", 0.1, 1.3),
    ("q-ai-nn-045", "c-ai-nn-006", "Summary", "What is Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)?", ["Techniques that fine-tune only a small number of (extra) model parameters while freezing most pre-trained weights.", "Methods that significantly reduce the memory and compute required for fine-tuning Large Language Models.", "Techniques like LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) and Prompt Tuning.", "A method that prunes the network down to 1% of its original size.", "A way to train a model from scratch efficiently."], [0, 1, 2], "PEFT methods (like LoRA) freeze the base model and train a tiny subset of parameters, saving massive amounts of memory and compute.", 0.3, 1.4),
    ("q-ai-nn-046", "c-ai-nn-006", "Summary", "How does LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) work?", ["It injects trainable low-rank decomposition matrices into the layers of the pre-trained model.", "It freezes the original pre-trained weights.", "It computes weight updates $\\Delta W$ as the product of two smaller matrices.", "It trains the model using only low-resolution images.", "It replaces the attention mechanism with a recurrent loop."], [0, 1, 2], "LoRA approximates weight updates using low-rank matrix decomposition (A*B), keeping the original weights frozen, significantly reducing trainable parameters.", 0.4, 1.5),
    ("q-ai-nn-047", "c-ai-nn-006", "Intuitive", "When fine-tuning a pre-trained model, why is it common practice to use a much smaller learning rate than during pre-training?", ["To prevent the pre-trained weights from being destroyed or heavily distorted (catastrophic forgetting).", "Because the model is already close to a good minimum for the general feature space.", "To make the training process run faster.", "Because large learning rates will cause the frozen layers to unfreeze.", "To ensure the new classification head learns slower than the base network."], [0, 1], "Pre-trained models are already in a good region of parameter space. Large learning rates would violently update the weights, destroying the useful pre-learned features (catastrophic forgetting).", 1.2, 1.6),
